<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopian Empire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ethiopian Empire was the Christian monarchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> that lasted from the restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a> in 1270 until the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> in 1974. It survived the Adal invasion in the sixteenth century, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896 and, after Italian occupation, was restored in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire.jpg" alt="Ethiopian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dag · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ethiopia_(1897%E2%80%931974).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a> fell in the 10th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian Highlands</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a>, Agaw rulers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a> region. Later church texts denied that the dynasty had pure Solomonic descent, and most Christians regarded the Zagwe as usurpers, although their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">rock-hewn churches</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a> were the high point of a tradition begun in late Aksumite times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> nobleman from Bete Amhara, rebelled with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>, which he promised a semi-independent standing, and defeated the Zagwe king at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ansata">Battle of Ansata</a>.</p><p>He was on the throne by 1270. He claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dil-naod">Dil Na'od</a>, the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, and so, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, from Menelik I. This claim was set out in the 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a>. Yekuno Amlak kept friendly relations with Muslim neighbours, writing to the Mamluk Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, and built the church of Genneta Maryam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a> (1314–1344) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawit-i">Dawit I</a> (1382–1413) the empire expanded and for a time dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>. It reached its peak under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> (1434–1468), who built churches and monasteries, encouraged literature and replaced regional warlords with administrative officials. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> then threatened the empire, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of Imam Ahmed Gran ended in his defeat in 1543 with Portuguese help. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo-migrations">Oromo migrations</a> cost the empire much of its southern territory, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and occupied parts of the north from 1557. In the 1630s Fasilides founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>, beginning the Gondarine period of relative peace.</p><p>After the deaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii">Iyasu II</a> in 1755 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyoas-i">Iyoas I</a> in 1769 came the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemene-mesafint">Zemene Mesafint</a>, when regional warlords held the real power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> (r. 1855–1868) ended it and died during the British Expedition to Abyssinia. Menelik II, from Shewa, annexed lands to the west, south and east, so that by 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> had its modern borders, and in 1896 his victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. Italy occupied the country after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italo-ethiopian-war">Second Italo-Ethiopian War</a> of 1935, using mustard gas, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> went into exile in May 1936. On 27 August 1942 he abolished the legal basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Eritrea was federated with Ethiopia under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> resolution of 2 December 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wollo-famine">Wollo famine</a>, discontent in the cities and high fuel prices led to a revolt in February 1974. A committee of officers known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> dismantled the imperial government and deposed and imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> on 12 September 1974, ending the monarchy. Geʿez literature, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a> among its works, had its most productive period under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>. The 1931 and 1955 constitutions introduced a parliament of two chambers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ethiopian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultanate belonged to a long tradition of Muslim polities in the Horn of Africa competing with the Christian <b>Ethiopian Empire</b> for control of the highland trade routes and the pastoral lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Expansion soon collided with the independent <b>Ethiopian Empire</b>, and it ended in defeat at Adwa in 1896, where Emperor Menelik II routed the army of General Oreste Baratieri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The war between the Christian <b>Ethiopian Empire</b> and Muslim Adal lasted from 1529 to 1543.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
